The display's description from the virtual device where the monitor has none

tv-emu's launcher starts the VM without QEMU's description of its monitor
(EDID), since that one names a mode of 75 pictures a second first. The
connector's "edid" file is then empty, and with it the list of resolutions
the television supports and everything else read from the description. The
virtual device has a description of its own in /etc/tvemu/edid (a television
with 1920x1080 at 60 as its own mode), which is read when the connector gives
none.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [tv-emu virtual machine]

--- a/dshalUtils.c
+++ b/dshalUtils.c
@@ -455,6 +455,17 @@
         fclose(edidFile);
 
         if (*length <= 0) {
+            /* tv-emu: the VM's monitor may come without a description (the
+             * launcher leaves QEMU's out, whose first mode is not one of a
+             * television). The virtual device has one of its own. */
+            FILE *ownFile = fopen("/etc/tvemu/edid", "rb");
+            if (ownFile) {
+                *length = (int)fread(edid, 1, DSHALUTILS_EDID_MAX_BYTES, ownFile);
+                fclose(ownFile);
+            }
+        }
+
+        if (*length <= 0) {
             closedir(drmClass);
             return -1;
         }
